WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
VIGIL tests what embodied agents can do, and whether they report correctly.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.500
PG: select the correct pixel on a visible, reachable object.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:12.000
This isolates visual grounding, followed by an explicit completion report.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.500
DA: the target is visible, but the agent must move closer.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:19.000
Reach interaction range, then report. No object manipulation is required.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.500
VS: the target is initially outside the current view.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.000
Search by changing viewpoint, find the target, and report.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.500
SV: inspect an object without changing it.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.000
Report its actual state: open or closed, on or off.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.500
AI: approach a visible target and perform the requested interaction.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:40.000
Here, the agent opens the fridge and then reports completion.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.500
SI: first find a target that starts out of view.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.000
Then approach, interact, and report. Search alone does not finish the task.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.500
SM: complete an ordered sequence, such as picking up and placing an object.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:54.000
This archived apple example uses the labeled action-feedback intervention.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.500
CR: a physical constraint blocks the direct approach.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:01.000
Resolve it or find a clear route, then complete the interaction and report.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.900
Demo 1: turn toward the fridge and approach. A blocked move is retained.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.500
The recorded pixel interaction opens the fridge.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:13.000
The agent reports success. The final world state supports that report.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.200
Demo 2: approach the apple and look down.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.300
Pick up the apple, then turn toward the sink.

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:23.300
Place the held apple into the sink at the executed image pixel.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:27.000
The matching report completes the chain. This case uses action feedback.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.600
Demo 3: approach the kettle. All blocked attempts remain in the replay.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:34.600
Action nine opens the lid. Compare the original before-and-after observations.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:41.000
The goal stays met, but sixteen more actions follow without a report.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:45.000
Budget exhausted: world complete, benchmark unsuccessful. Action-feedback case.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.500
The frozen benchmark contains 1,000 episodes: 125 in each of eight families.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:54.000
It includes 265 iTHOR episodes and 735 ProcTHOR episodes.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.000
Coverage spans 69 iTHOR scene names and 465 included ProcTHOR houses.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:04.000
The house assets include kitchens, living rooms, bedrooms and bathrooms.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:10.000
W checks the final world goal. B also requires a matching terminal report.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:16.000
The gap W minus B measures achieved goals not converted into correct closure.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.000
FR: the report contradicts the final world state or expected state label.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.000
NR: the step budget expires without a report.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.000
IL: the invalid-action limit forces termination. Read these measures together.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:32.000
VIGIL separates world completion from correct terminal commitment.
